# tighConaill -- Conall's morning, in his cabin: hello
#
# The player wakes in his bed after the landfall. A word of greeting and a word about the
# light; then the scene sends him to the window to draw the curtain (no note: it is just
# what happens when this conversation ends). Read at the player's own pace.
#
# IRISH IS A FIRST DRAFT -- check before shipping.

@file skye/tighConaill
@encounter 0


# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
= node 0 — awake
@continue




# skyeLoch -- Meeting Conall after the sea

@file skye/conallHearth
@encounter 0

= rescued

> Drowning!
% Ag bá!

> The black water has me,
% ga_2: Tá mé i ngreim ag an uisce dubh,

> It fills my throat.
% ga_1: líonan sé mo scornach.

< Lie still.
% ga_2: Bí socair.

> I was drowning. I was surely drowning.
% ga_1: Bhí mé  báite. Bhí mé báite gan amhras.

< Thou art weary, nothing more.
% ga_1: Níl ort ach tuirse.

> Where is this place?
% ga_2: Cá bhfuil mé anois?

< Thou art my guest. I am Conall.
% ga_1: Mo aoi thú. Conall is ainm dom.

< And this is the mark of a student of Dún Scáthach. It is thine.
% ga_1: Chaitheann dhaltí Dhún Scáthach íad seo. Is leatsa é.

> I bring no cattle, no fine tribute of silver.
% ga_1: Níor thugas liom eallach, ná tiarnas breá airgid.

< Ease now thy troubled mind
% Bí socair

< Thou hast made thy case already upon the shore, senseless and half-drowned,
% ga_1: Rinne tú do chás cheana féin nuair a bhain tú amach an trá,  gan aithne, leathbáite,

< with the sea still running from thee.
% ga_1: agus an fharraige fós ag sileadh uait.

< Take thy rest,
% ga_1: Lig do scíth,

< Then shall I, set thee on the path of the blade,
% ga_1: Ansin cuirfidh mé ar bhóthar an chlaímh thú,

< Is that not why all Gaelic Chieftains come to Skye?
% ga_1: Nach é sin a tógann na Taoisigh Gaelach ar fad go Cheo?








# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
= node 1 — the light


< Thou hast slept the better part of two days,
% ga_1: Chodail tú dhá lá nach mór.

